{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792413414,"nonce":"+e9nUeXHoa01CgmTof9N13qQpag=","title":"A macro that never finishes hangs the compile, and with it the language server","message":"Measured 2026-10-19 with a scripted LSP client on the NUC: opening a file whose `@:build` macro is `while (true) {}` gave no diagnostic within 20 s, and the server never answered another request. Throwing and endlessly recursing macros were already handled: the recursion limit is 256 and becomes E0700. `rayzor run` on such a file hangs as well, as haxe does.\n\nFix: macro_system::budget, a per-thread time budget checked on every loop iteration and call of the interpreter and on the bytecode VM's dispatch loop (clock read once per 1024 checks). It produces TimeBudgetExceeded (E0713), which a macro's own try/catch cannot swallow. The language server sets 5 s per compile (`initializationOptions.macroBudgetMs`); the command line sets none.","files":null}]}